<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicholas II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">abdicated <a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicholas II was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, who abdicated in 1917. After the 1905 revolution he issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a> creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a> but consistently undermined its authority. His personal command of the army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> tied the dynasty to military failure, and he abdicated in March 1917 amid revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii.jpg" alt="Nicholas II" width="301" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (originally uploaded to Wikimedia Commons on 22 August 2012 by Peruanec) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikola_II_(cropped)-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nicholas II was born on 18 May 1868 (6 May in the Old Style calendar) at the Alexander Palace in Tsarskoye Selo, the eldest child of the future Emperor Alexander III and Maria Feodorovna, born Princess Dagmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. He was educated privately and trained for military service, though he was widely considered ill-prepared to rule. He became heir apparent on 1 March 1881, when his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> was assassinated by the Narodnaya Volya. In April 1894 he proposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandra-feodorovna-alix-of-hesse">Alix of Hesse</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, and they were engaged on 20 April. He became emperor on 1 November 1894, when his father died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nicholas believed that his authority came from God and that his duty was to keep his absolute power intact. Early in his reign a deputation of zemstvo representatives presented the Tver Address, proposing constitutional reform, and he rejected it. He pursued an aggressive policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>; defeat, together with the demonstrations shot on Bloody Sunday in January 1905, fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a>. On 5 August 1905 he issued a manifesto convening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a> he pledged civil liberties and a Duma with legislative powers. The 1906 constitution nevertheless described him as the supreme autocrat, and ministers answered only to him.</p><p>In 1904 his son Alexei was born, who had haemophilia, an illness that complicated the question of the succession. In 1907 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> settled central Asian disputes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-entente">Triple Entente</a>. The government condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Kishinev</a> pogrom, though in private Nicholas reportedly admired the mobs. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> measures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, begun with the February Manifesto of 1899, were deeply unpopular.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the First World War Nicholas took command at the front in 1915, leaving the capital to intrigue and strikes, and the failure to supply the army and the population brought riots and mutinies. He abdicated in March 1917, ending 304 years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> rule, and tried to pass the throne to his brother Michael.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> offered the family asylum on 19 March 1917 but withdrew the offer in April. The family was held first at Tsarskoye Selo, then in Bolshevik custody, and was executed at Yekaterinburg on 17 July 1918. Their remains were reburied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1998, and in 2000 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> canonised them as passion bearers. The majority view among historians is that he was well-intentioned but a poor ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicholas II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The October Manifesto of 1905, issued under revolutionary pressure, and the Fundamental Laws of 1906 created the State Duma as a legislative assembly with genuine if limited representative authority, a constitutional order that <b>Nicholas II</b> consistently undermined in practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The French Revolution ended absolute monarchy in France in 1789; the revolutions of 1848 led Denmark to give up absolutism and Prussia to accept a constitution; and after the Russian Revolution of 1905 <b>Nicholas II</b>’s October Manifesto promised an elected Duma.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Royal Scots Dragoon Guards still play it at formal events in tribute to <b>Nicholas II</b>, who had been colonel-in-chief of their predecessor regiment, the Scots Greys.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1917, after Tsar <b>Nicholas II</b> had abdicated, Sergei Diaghilev asked Igor Stravinsky to orchestrate it for wind instruments to open a concert that would otherwise have begun with the anthem &quot;God Save the Tsar&quot;; Stravinsky worked through the night, helped by Lord Berners and Ernest Ansermet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li><li><blockquote>Imperial troops fired on unarmed workers led by Father Georgy Gapon marching to petition Tsar <b>Nicholas II</b>, killing many.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-22">22 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early hours, <b>Nicholas II</b>, his wife, their five children and four retainers were shot in the cellar of the Ipatiev House.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pushkin, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Ipatiev House, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.72,30.42&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Pushkin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5188 160 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicholas II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5638 430h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5638 430h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.72,30.42&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
